from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('topic-14','devices','semiconductor-physics','Semiconductor Physics · Bands & Equilibrium','007');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For a nondegenerate semiconductor, n/ni=exp[(EF−Ei)/(kT)]. If EF−Ei=kT ln10, find n/ni.',10,'Nondegenerate approximation; specified Fermi-level shift','Find normalized electron concentration.','Boltzmann carrier concentration',r'n/n_i=e^{(E_F-E_i)/(kT)}',r'The dimensionless exponent is ln10. | Exponentiation reverses the natural logarithm. | The concentration ratio is 10.','Use the natural logarithm with an exponential base e.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'At equilibrium with complete donor ionization, n−p=1.5×10¹⁰ cm⁻³ and np=10²⁰ cm⁻⁶. Find n in units of 10¹⁰ cm⁻³.',2,'Charge-neutrality difference 1.5×10¹⁰; mass-action product 10²⁰','Find the positive majority-electron solution in scaled units.','Exact carrier neutrality',r'n^2-N_Dn-n_i^2=0',r'Write n=a×10¹⁰ and p=b×10¹⁰, so a−b=1.5 and ab=1. | Substituting b=a−1.5 gives a²−1.5a−1=0, with roots 2 and −0.5. | Only the positive root is physical: n=2×10¹⁰ cm⁻³ and p=0.5×10¹⁰ cm⁻³.','Do not approximate n by donor density when donor density is comparable to ni.',difficulty='hard',unit='×10¹⁰ cm⁻³')
b.n(r'Use ni=sqrt(NcNv) exp[−Eg/(2kT)]. For Nc=4×10¹⁹ cm⁻³, Nv=10¹⁹ cm⁻³ and Eg=20kT, find ni in units of 10¹⁴ cm⁻³.',9.07998595249697,'Stated Boltzmann density model; Eg/(2kT)=10','Find intrinsic concentration in requested scaled units.','Intrinsic density from band edges',r'n_i=\sqrt{N_cN_v}e^{-E_g/(2kT)}',r'The geometric-mean density is √(4×10³⁸)=2×10¹⁹ cm⁻³. | Multiply by e⁻¹⁰≈4.539992976×10⁻⁵ to obtain 9.079985952×10¹⁴ cm⁻³. | The requested numerical value is 9.079985952.','The band gap appears divided by 2kT for intrinsic concentration, not kT.',unit='×10¹⁴ cm⁻³')
b.m(r'The Fermi–Dirac occupation probability at energy E=EF at a positive temperature is:','B',['0','1/2','1','Temperature in kelvin'],'Fermi–Dirac distribution; E equals chemical potential','Find the occupation probability.','Fermi occupation',r'f(E)=1/(1+e^{(E-E_F)/(kT)})',r'The exponent is zero at E=EF. | Its exponential is 1. | Occupation is 1/(1+1)=1/2.','Occupation probability is not the total density of available states.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Using the Fermi–Dirac law, find the occupation probability at E−EF=kT ln3.',0.25,'Positive dimensionless energy offset ln3','Evaluate the occupation function.','Fermi–Dirac denominator',r'f=1/(1+e^{(E-E_F)/(kT)})',r'The exponential term is exp(ln3)=3. | The full denominator is 1+3=4. | Thus f=1/4=0.25.','The Boltzmann approximation would omit the 1; this question asks for the exact stated law.')
b.n(r'A compensated n-type semiconductor has fully ionized ND=5×10¹⁶ and NA=2×10¹⁶ cm⁻³. Neglect holes in charge neutrality. Find n in units of 10¹⁶ cm⁻³.',3,'Complete dopant ionization; holes explicitly neglected','Find net majority concentration.','Donor–acceptor compensation',r'n\simeq N_D-N_A',r'Positive donor charge is partly balanced by negatively ionized acceptors. | The remainder is balanced by conduction electrons: 5×10¹⁶−2×10¹⁶. | n≈3×10¹⁶ cm⁻³, so enter 3.','Use net ionized doping rather than total dopant count.',unit='×10¹⁶ cm⁻³')
b.s(r'Which descriptions of semiconductor energy bands are correct?',['A','C','D'],['An intrinsic semiconductor can thermally generate electron–hole pairs','The forbidden gap contains a continuum of allowed bulk Bloch states in an ideal perfect crystal','A direct-gap material has conduction minimum and valence maximum at the same crystal wavevector','Band-gap energy is an energy separation, not a carrier concentration'],'Ideal bulk band picture; no defect levels in the forbidden gap','Select valid band-structure facts.','Band gap and thermal excitation',r'E_g=E_{c,min}-E_{v,max}',r'Thermal excitation can move an electron from a valence state into a conduction state, leaving a hole. | A forbidden ideal bulk gap lacks the claimed allowed-state continuum. | Direct-gap extrema share wavevector, and Eg is an energy difference. Select A, C and D.','Defect states are not the same as an allowed-band continuum in an ideal perfect crystal.')
b.n(r'A material has absorption threshold wavelength λg=1240 nm under Eg(eV)=1240/λg(nm). Find Eg in eV.',1,'Threshold relation hc=1240 eV·nm; λg=1240 nm','Find the optical gap.','Photon energy threshold',r'E_g=hc/\lambda_g',r'The threshold photon energy equals the band-gap energy in the stated approximation. | Divide 1240 eV·nm by 1240 nm. | Eg=1 eV.','A longer threshold wavelength corresponds to a smaller gap, not a larger gap.',unit='eV')
b.n(r'In a nondegenerate n-type semiconductor n/ni=100 and VT=kT/q=0.026 V. Find (EF−Ei)/q in volts.',0.1197344248356904,'Electron density ratio 100; thermal voltage 0.026 V','Find normalized Fermi-level displacement.','Fermi-level logarithmic shift',r'(E_F-E_i)/q=V_T\ln(n/n_i)',r'Take natural logarithms of the Boltzmann concentration relation. | ln100≈4.605170186. | Multiply by 0.026 V to obtain approximately 0.119734425 V.','The logarithm is natural; VT already includes division by electron charge.',unit='V')
b.m(r'In ordinary n-type material with complete donor ionization and negligible compensation, the majority carriers are:','C',['Holes only','Ionized donors moving through the lattice','Conduction electrons','Valence-band vacancies counted as negative ions'],'Standard n-type majority-carrier terminology','Identify mobile majority carriers.','Doping and mobile charge',r'n\gg p',r'Donors provide electrons that can occupy mobile conduction-band states. | The ionized donor atoms remain bound to lattice sites in the ordinary model. | The majority mobile carriers are therefore conduction electrons, option C.','Fixed ionized dopants contribute space charge but are not ordinary mobile current carriers.')
b.s(r'Which assumptions are used when writing n≈ND for a uniformly donor-doped semiconductor?',['A','B','D'],['Donors are substantially ionized','Compensation by acceptors is negligible','Intrinsic carriers must dominate the doping','Minority holes are negligible in the charge-neutrality balance'],'Uniform equilibrium sample; approximate majority relation','Select required approximation conditions.','Majority-carrier approximation',r'n+N_A^-=p+N_D^+',r'The exact charge-neutrality relation balances mobile and dopant charges. | To reduce it to n≈ND, take ND+≈ND, NA−≈0 and p≪n. | Intrinsic domination would invalidate the approximation, so select A, B and D.','Check the ionization and intrinsic regimes before using the common n≈ND shortcut.')
b.n(r'At fixed temperature in the nondegenerate model, raising EF by kT ln4 changes n by a factor 4. By what factor does p change?',0.25,'Thermal equilibrium; same temperature and ni; Fermi shift kTln4','Find new-hole/old-hole concentration ratio.','Equilibrium mass-action compensation',r'np=n_i^2\quad(T\text{ fixed})',r'The intrinsic product remains constant when temperature and material are unchanged. | If n increases fourfold, p must decrease fourfold to keep np fixed. | Therefore pnew/pold=1/4=0.25.','Increasing both electron and hole equilibrium densities independently would violate mass action.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-15','devices','carrier-transport','Carrier Transport · Currents, Lifetime & Fields','007');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'In the low-field drift model, μn=1000 cm²/(V·s) and E=+100 V/cm. Find the signed electron drift velocity along x in cm/s.',-100000,'Positive electric field; electron mobility positive','Find electron particle drift velocity.','Electron drift direction',r'v_{dn}=-\mu_nE',r'Electrons have negative charge and drift opposite to the field. | Multiply mobility by field: 1000×100=10⁵ cm/s. | Include the direction sign to obtain −10⁵ cm/s.','Conventional electron current points opposite to electron particle drift.',unit='cm/s',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A semiconductor has n=p=5×10¹⁴ cm⁻³, μn=2000 and μp=400 cm²/(V·s). At E=10 V/cm, find total drift current density in A/cm² using q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C.',1.92,'Both carrier densities specified; uniform low field; no diffusion term requested','Add electron and hole conventional drift currents.','Two-carrier drift conductivity',r'J=q(n\mu_n+p\mu_p)E',r'The weighted carrier mobility sum is 5×10¹⁴(2000+400)=1.2×10¹⁸. | Multiplying by q gives conductivity 0.192 S/cm. | Multiplying by E=10 V/cm gives J=1.92 A/cm².','Electron and hole drift currents add in the conventional current reference despite opposite particle drifts.',unit='A/cm²')
b.n(r'Minority carriers have diffusion coefficient D=25 cm²/s and lifetime τ=4 μs. Find the diffusion length in μm.',100,'D=25 cm²/s; τ=4×10⁻⁶ s','Find √(Dτ) and convert units.','Diffusion length',r'L=\sqrt{D\tau}',r'Dτ=25×4×10⁻⁶=10⁻⁴ cm². | Taking its square root gives 10⁻² cm. | Multiplying by 10⁴ μm/cm gives 100 μm.','Lifetime must be in seconds before multiplication by a cm²/s coefficient.',unit='μm')
b.n(r'Uniform excess carriers obey dΔn/dt=−Δn/τ after illumination stops. With τ=2 μs, find the time at which the excess density falls to one quarter, in μs.',2.772588722239781,'First-order recombination; initial excess positive; lifetime 2 μs','Find quarter-life time.','Excess-carrier decay',r'\Delta n(t)=\Delta n(0)e^{-t/\tau}',r'Set the decay factor to 1/4. | Taking logarithms gives t=τln4. | With τ=2 μs, t=2ln4≈2.772588722 μs.','A quarter density is reached after two half-lives, not a quarter of one lifetime.',unit='μs')
b.n(r'Uniform steady photogeneration is G=10²⁰ cm⁻³s⁻¹ and the linear excess-carrier lifetime is 1 μs. Ignoring transport, find the excess steady density in units of 10¹⁴ cm⁻³.',1,'Uniform G; recombination Δn/τ; steady state','Find excess generation–recombination balance.','Continuity at steady state',r'0=G-\Delta n/\tau',r'No spatial transport or time variation remains under the stated assumptions. | Set generation equal to recombination to obtain Δn=Gτ=10²⁰×10⁻⁶=10¹⁴ cm⁻³. | In requested scaled units, enter 1.','The result is excess concentration, not necessarily the total equilibrium-plus-excess density.',unit='×10¹⁴ cm⁻³')
b.m(r'A hole concentration decreases in the +x direction with zero electric field. The conventional hole diffusion current points:','A',['In +x','In −x','Nowhere because E=0','In a direction independent of the concentration gradient'],'Positive hole charge; dp/dx<0; diffusion only','Find the sign of hole diffusion current.','Hole diffusion sign',r'J_{p,diff}=-qD_p\frac{dp}{dx}',r'Particles diffuse from higher to lower concentration, here in +x. | Holes carry positive charge, so their conventional current has that same direction. | The formula also gives a positive value when dp/dx<0, so A is correct.','A zero electric field removes drift, not diffusion.')
b.n(r'For p(x)=p0+10¹⁵exp(−x/0.001) cm⁻³ with x in cm, Dp=10 cm²/s and q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C, find Jp,diff(0) in A/cm².',1.6,'Excess-hole profile; diffusion coefficient 10; length 0.001 cm','Differentiate the profile and calculate current.','Hole-profile diffusion current',r'J_p=-qD_p\,dp/dx',r'At x=0, dp/dx=−10¹⁵/0.001=−10¹⁸ cm⁻⁴. | Multiply by −qDp=−1.6×10⁻¹⁹×10. | The product is +1.6 A/cm², directed along +x.','The length in the exponential denominator contributes to the derivative.',unit='A/cm²',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A uniform sample has conductivity 1 S/cm, length 1 cm and cross-sectional area 10⁻⁴ cm². Neglect contacts. Find resistance in kΩ.',10,'σ=1 S/cm; L=1 cm; A=10⁻⁴ cm²','Find L/(σA).','Bulk conduction geometry',r'R=L/(\sigma A)',r'The sample conductance is σA/L=10⁻⁴ S. | Resistance is its reciprocal, 10⁴ Ω. | Convert to kΩ to obtain 10 kΩ.','Conductivity is a material property; conductance also depends on geometry.',unit='kΩ')
b.n(r'In a fully ionized extrinsic regime, hold carrier density fixed and assume μ(T)∝T⁻³ᐟ². Find σ(600 K)/σ(300 K) in this model.',0.3535533905932738,'Fixed carrier density; mobility power law only; temperature doubles','Find conductivity ratio.','Mobility-limited temperature dependence',r'\sigma_2/\sigma_1=(T_2/T_1)^{-3/2}',r'With density fixed, conductivity changes in direct proportion to mobility. | The temperature ratio is 2. | The model gives 2⁻³ᐟ²=1/(2√2)≈0.353553391.','This model excludes intrinsic-density increase and dopant freeze-out effects.')
b.n(r'For a 1D semiconductor region, dE/dx=10⁵ V/cm² and ε=10⁻¹² F/cm. Using q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C, find net positive charge concentration ρ/q in units of 10¹¹ cm⁻³.',6.25,'One-dimensional Poisson relation; field gradient positive','Find net charge density then normalize by q.','Poisson charge relation',r'\frac{dE}{dx}=\rho/\epsilon',r'The charge density is ε(dE/dx)=10⁻¹²×10⁵=10⁻⁷ C/cm³. | Divide by 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C to obtain 6.25×10¹¹ cm⁻³. | Enter 6.25 in the requested units. This is net charge concentration, not automatically an electron density.','The net charge can include fixed dopants and both carrier types.',unit='×10¹¹ cm⁻³',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'Which statements about low-field semiconductor drift/diffusion models are true?',['A','C','D'],['A concentration gradient can produce current even when the electric field is zero','Electron particle flux and electron conventional current always point in the same direction','The Einstein relation links mobility and diffusion under nondegenerate equilibrium assumptions','A spatially uniform concentration removes its diffusion term'],'Conventional current; scalar positive mobilities/diffusivities','Select correct transport distinctions.','Particle flux versus charge current',r'J_n=q n\mu_nE+qD_n\nabla n',r'The gradient term can survive at zero field and vanishes for uniform density. | Multiplying electron particle flux by negative electron charge reverses its direction. | Nondegenerate equilibrium assumptions give D/μ=kT/q. Select A, C and D.','The sign convention must follow carrier charge, not just particle motion.')
b.s(r'For uniform linear recombination model dΔn/dt=G−Δn/τ with τ>0, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['The steady excess concentration is Gτ for constant generation','With G=0, excess density decays exponentially','τ has units of inverse seconds','The model’s homogeneous pole is −1/τ'],'Constant G; lifetime τ; no transport','Identify units and dynamics.','Linear carrier continuity',r'\Delta n(t)=G\tau+C e^{-t/\tau}',r'Steady balance gives Δn=Gτ. | Setting G=0 leaves a decaying exponential with pole −1/τ. | Since t/τ is dimensionless, τ has units of time. Select A, B and D.','Lifetime is a time, whereas recombination rate coefficient 1/τ has inverse-time units.')

b=Bank('topic-16','devices','pn-junctions','PN Junctions · Depletion, Bias & Charge Storage','007');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A planar abrupt junction has area 10⁻⁴ cm², permittivity 10⁻¹² F/cm and depletion width 10⁻⁴ cm. Find its depletion capacitance in pF.',1,'A=10⁻⁴ cm²; ε=10⁻¹² F/cm; W=10⁻⁴ cm','Find εA/W.','Junction depletion capacitance',r'C_j=\epsilon A/W',r'The permittivity-area product is 10⁻¹⁶ F·cm. | Divide by W=10⁻⁴ cm to obtain 10⁻¹² F. | This equals 1 pF.','All length and area units must use the same centimeter convention.',unit='pF',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For an abrupt junction with built-in voltage Vbi=1 V, assume Cj(VR)=Cj(0)/sqrt(1+VR/Vbi). Find Cj(3 V)/Cj(0).',0.5,'Abrupt-junction reverse-capacitance model; VR=3 V','Find the normalized reverse-bias capacitance.','Reverse-bias depletion expansion',r'C_j(V_R)/C_j(0)=(1+V_R/V_{bi})^{-1/2}',r'Inside the square root the ratio is 1+3/1=4. | The square root is 2. | The capacitance ratio is 1/2, corresponding to twice the depletion width at fixed area.','Increasing reverse bias widens depletion and lowers junction capacitance.')
b.n(r'An ideal diode has IS=1 nA, η=1 and VT=25 mV. Find the forward voltage for I=10 μA, in millivolts, retaining the −1 term in the diode law.',230.26100917441287,'I/IS=10000; thermal voltage 25 mV','Invert the full diode current relation.','Diode inverse exponential',r'V=\eta V_T\ln(1+I/I_S)',r'Rearrange I=IS(exp(V/VT)−1) to exp(V/VT)=10001. | Multiply ln10001 by 25 mV. | The required voltage is approximately 230.261009 mV.','The exact logarithm argument is 1+I/IS, not simply I/IS.',unit='mV')
b.n(r'In an abrupt junction depletion approximation, NA=10¹⁷ and ND=10¹⁶ cm⁻³. Find xp/xn.',0.1,'Uniform ionized doping on each side; depletion charge neutrality','Find the ratio of p-side to n-side widths.','Depletion charge balance',r'N_Ax_p=N_Dx_n',r'The integrated positive and negative depletion charges must have equal magnitude. | Divide the balance by NAxn to obtain xp/xn=ND/NA. | The ratio is 10¹⁶/10¹⁷=0.1.','Depletion extends farther into the more lightly doped side.')
b.n(r'For NA=10¹⁷, ND=10¹⁶ and ni=10¹⁰ cm⁻³ at VT=0.025 V, find the equilibrium built-in voltage in volts using the nondegenerate model.',0.7483401552230649,'Abrupt equilibrium PN junction; fully ionized nondegenerate doping','Find VT ln(NAND/ni²).','Built-in electrostatic potential',r'V_{bi}=V_T\ln(N_AN_D/n_i^2)',r'The concentration ratio inside the logarithm is 10³³/10²⁰=10¹³. | Its natural logarithm is 13ln10≈29.93360621. | Multiply by 0.025 V to obtain 0.748340155 V.','The built-in potential is an internal electrostatic difference, not a usable equilibrium battery EMF.',unit='V',difficulty='hard')
b.m(r'At thermal equilibrium in an ideal PN junction, which statement is correct?','D',['The depletion electric field must vanish','There are no mobile carriers anywhere','Electron diffusion current alone must vanish everywhere','The net terminal current is zero because transport contributions balance'],'No applied bias; thermal equilibrium','Identify the equilibrium current constraint.','Drift–diffusion balance',r'J_{total}=J_n+J_p=0',r'The built-in field and carrier gradients can both be nonzero. | Their corresponding transport contributions balance at equilibrium. | The net terminal current is zero, without requiring each individual drift or diffusion contribution to vanish. Select D.','Equilibrium does not mean the absence of every microscopic transport component.')
b.n(r'Using charge-control model Q=τI with constant transit-storage time τ=10 ns, forward current I=1 mA and ideality factor 1 at VT=25 mV, use the high-forward-bias approximation dI/dV≈I/VT to find diffusion capacitance dQ/dV in pF.',400,'Q=τI; τ constant; exponential forward-current slope I/VT','Find τ times incremental diode conductance.','Diffusion charge capacitance',r'C_d=\tau\,dI/dV\simeq\tau I/V_T',r'The stated forward-current slope is 0.001/0.025=0.04 S. | Multiply by storage time 10⁻⁸ s to get 4×10⁻¹⁰ F. | This is 400 pF. The model concerns stored minority charge rather than depletion width.','Depletion capacitance and diffusion capacitance represent different stored charges.',unit='pF',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'An ideal diode follows I=IS(exp(V/VT)−1). At V=−10VT and IS=1 nA, find I in nA.',-0.9999546000702375,'Reverse voltage −10VT; saturation current 1 nA; no breakdown','Find the signed reverse current.','Reverse exponential limit',r'I/I_S=e^{-10}-1',r'The exponential is e⁻¹⁰≈0.00004539993. | Subtract 1 to obtain −0.9999546001. | Since IS=1 nA, current is −0.9999546001 nA, close to −IS.','The reverse current has negative sign in the forward-current reference.',unit='nA')
b.n(r'A measured reverse-recovery current is a triangle with magnitude rising from zero to 2 mA and returning to zero over total duration 4 ns. Find the recovered charge magnitude in pC.',4,'Triangular reverse-current magnitude; total base 4 ns; peak 2 mA','Integrate the transient current magnitude.','Reverse-recovery charge area',r'Q_{rr}=\int|i_r|dt=\tfrac12 I_{pk}t_{base}',r'The magnitude-time area is half the triangle’s base times height. | ½×2×10⁻³ A×4×10⁻⁹ s=4×10⁻¹² C. | The recovered charge magnitude is 4 pC.','Use the complete stated triangle duration, not one half of it.',unit='pC')
b.s(r'Which statements about an ideal abrupt reverse-biased PN junction before breakdown are correct?',['A','C','D'],['Depletion width generally increases with reverse bias','The depletion region becomes perfectly conducting','Junction capacitance generally decreases as depletion widens','Space charge arises primarily from uncovered ionized dopants in the depletion approximation'],'Abrupt junction; reverse bias below breakdown; depletion model','Select valid depletion-region properties.','Reverse-bias depletion electrostatics',r'W\propto\sqrt{V_{bi}+V_R},\quad C_j=\epsilon A/W',r'Greater reverse potential requires a wider space-charge region in this model. | Its small-signal capacitance decreases because separation W increases. | The depleted mobile carriers leave fixed dopant ions; this is not a perfectly conducting region. Select A, C and D.','Depleted carrier density does not imply zero electric field.')
b.n(r'A junction has total depletion width W=1.1 μm and NA/ND=10. Find the n-side depletion width xn in μm.',1,'xp+xn=1.1 μm; NAxp=NDxn; doping ratio 10','Find the more lightly doped side’s width.','Combined depletion width constraints',r'x_p=x_n/10,\quad W=x_p+x_n',r'Charge balance gives xp=xn/10. | Substitute into total width: 1.1=1.1xn in μm. | Thus xn=1 μm and xp=0.1 μm.','Do not split the width equally for unequal dopings.',unit='μm')
b.s(r'At equilibrium in a semiconductor PN junction at uniform temperature, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['The Fermi level is spatially constant','Built-in electric field can be nonzero','The net external short-circuit current is a sustained power source','The built-in field and diffusion effects can balance'],'Thermal equilibrium; no illumination or applied excitation','Select equilibrium-band-diagram properties.','Electrochemical equilibrium',r'\nabla E_F=0\quad\text{at thermal equilibrium}',r'A constant equilibrium Fermi level reflects no net electrochemical driving force. | Band bending and an internal field can coexist with this equilibrium because diffusion balances drift. | Such a junction cannot deliver sustained external power at equilibrium. Select A, B and D.','An internal electrostatic barrier is not evidence of a perpetual DC source.')

b=Bank('mixed-09','mathematics','probability-and-statistics','Mixed Revision · Moments, Timing & Matching','007');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A random variable takes values −1 and 3, each with probability 1/2. Find its standard deviation.',2,'Two equally likely observations −1,3','Find square root of variance.','Central second moment',r'\sigma=\sqrt{E[(X-E[X])^2]}',r'The mean is (−1+3)/2=1. | Both deviations from the mean have magnitude 2, giving variance 4. | Standard deviation is √4=2.','Standard deviation and variance have different units and numerical values.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A pulse has amplitude 2 on 0≤t≤3 s and is zero elsewhere. Find its energy ∫|x(t)|²dt.',12,'Rectangular pulse amplitude 2; duration 3 s','Find integrated squared magnitude.','Signal energy',r'E=\int|x(t)|^2dt',r'The squared amplitude is 4 while the pulse is present. | Integrate that constant over duration 3. | The energy measure is 4×3=12.','Energy integrates squared amplitude, not amplitude itself.',subject='networks',topic='signals-and-systems')
b.n(r'A synchronous path has minimum clock-to-Q delay 0.5 ns, minimum combinational delay 0.8 ns and hold requirement 1 ns. Ignoring skew, find its hold slack in ns.',0.3,'Minimum delays 0.5,0.8 ns; capture hold time 1 ns; zero skew','Find earliest data arrival minus required hold time.','Hold-time margin',r'S_{hold}=t_{cq,min}+t_{comb,min}-t_{hold}',r'The earliest new data arrives after 0.5+0.8=1.3 ns. | It must remain old for at least 1 ns. | Slack is 1.3−1=0.3 ns, positive under the stated convention.','Hold checking uses minimum path delays, whereas setup checking uses maximum delays.',subject='digital',topic='sequential-circuits',unit='ns')
b.n(r'In a diode model with ideality factor 2 and VT=25 mV, what forward voltage increment multiplies I+IS by e? Give millivolts.',50,'I+IS=IS exp[V/(ηVT)]; η=2; VT=25 mV','Find one natural-log current e-fold voltage.','Ideality-factor exponential slope',r'\Delta V=\eta V_T\ln(e)',r'The ratio of I+IS values at two biases is exp(ΔV/(ηVT)). | For ratio e the exponent must equal 1. | ΔV=2×25 mV=50 mV.','Ideality factor changes the voltage scale; it cannot be omitted.',subject='devices',topic='pn-junctions',unit='mV')
b.m(r'A negative-feedback op-amp voltage follower has ideal closed-loop signal gain:','A',['1','0','−1','Infinite'],'Output connected directly to inverting input; signal at noninverting input; linear operation','Find follower gain.','Unity feedback constraint',r'V_-=V_o,\quad V_+=V_i',r'Linear ideal negative feedback makes V+=V−. | With the output fed directly to V−, this equates Vo to Vi. | The gain is +1, option A; high open-loop gain enables the constraint rather than becoming the closed-loop gain.','A voltage follower changes buffering/impedance behavior even though voltage gain is unity.',subject='analog',topic='operational-amplifiers')
b.n(r'A unity-feedback loop has G(s)=12/(s+3). Find its unit-step steady-state error.',0.2,'Stable loop; forward DC gain 4','Find final error to a unit reference step.','Type-0 position error',r'e_{ss}=1/(1+K_p),\quad K_p=G(0)',r'The DC loop gain is 12/3=4. | The step-error limit is 1/(1+4). | Steady-state error is 0.2, while the output final value is 0.8.','Output value and tracking error sum to the unit reference but are not identical.',subject='control',topic='time-response')
b.n(r'A binary memoryless source has P(0)=1 and P(1)=0. Find its entropy in bits per symbol, using 0 log₂0=0 by limit.',0,'Deterministic source output','Find entropy.','Zero uncertainty',r'H=-\sum_i p_i\log_2p_i',r'The certain outcome contributes −1log₂1=0. | The impossible outcome contributes zero by the specified limiting convention. | Total entropy is 0 bits/symbol because there is no uncertainty.','A binary alphabet alone does not force one bit of entropy.',subject='communications',topic='information-theory',unit='bits/symbol',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A lossless line has |Γ|=0.2. Find its reflected-to-incident power ratio as a percentage.',4,'Same line/reference impedance for incident and reflected power waves','Find 100|Γ|².','Power reflection fraction',r'P_r/P_i=|\Gamma|^2',r'Voltage-wave amplitude ratio is 0.2. | Power in the same impedance is proportional to squared amplitude, so the ratio is 0.04. | As a percentage this is 4%.','Do not interpret a 20% amplitude reflection as a 20% power reflection.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='transmission-lines',unit='%')
b.s(r'For a finite-dimensional LTI state equation xdot=Ax with no input, which conditions guarantee asymptotic decay of every initial state?',['A','D'],['Every eigenvalue of A has strictly negative real part','trace(A)<0 alone for any dimension','det(A)>0 alone for any dimension','A is diagonal with strictly negative diagonal entries'],'Continuous-time unforced state dynamics','Select sufficient conditions for every initial state.','Internal asymptotic stability',r'e^{At}\to0\iff\operatorname{Re}\lambda_i(A)<0\ \forall i',r'The strict spectral condition makes all exponential/polynomial state modes decay. | A negative diagonal A is a special case satisfying it. | Trace or determinant signs alone do not constrain every eigenvalue in arbitrary dimension. Select A and D.','Scalar invariants alone are insufficient general stability tests.',subject='control',topic='state-space-analysis',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'Which 4-bit unsigned additions produce a carry out beyond bit 3?',['B','D'],['0011 + 0100','1111 + 0001','1000 + 0111','1010 + 1010'],'Unsigned 4-bit arithmetic with carry retained separately','Select sums exceeding 15.','Binary carry range',r'C_{out}=1\iff a+b\ge16',r'The decimal sums are 3+4=7, 15+1=16, 8+7=15 and 10+10=20. | Only the second and fourth exceed the 4-bit maximum 15. | Therefore B and D produce a carry out.','Unsigned carry and signed two’s-complement overflow are different flags.',subject='digital',topic='combinational-circuits')
b.n(r'A product’s marked price is 500 units. A 10% discount is followed by a 20% tax applied to the discounted price. Find the final price.',540,'Marked price 500; discount then tax on reduced base','Apply ordered percentage changes.','Sequential price factors',r'P_f=P_0(1-d)(1+t)',r'The discounted price is 500×0.9=450. | Tax is 20% of 450, which is 90. | The final price is 450+90=540.','The tax base is the discounted price, not the original marked price.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude')
b.n(r'Let f(z)=1/(z²+1). Find the imaginary part of its residue at the pole z=j.',-0.5,'Simple poles at ±j; residue requested at +j','Find Im[1/(2j)].','Complex simple-pole residue',r'\operatorname{Res}(f,j)=1/(2j)',r'Factor z²+1=(z−j)(z+j). | At z=j the residue is 1/(j+j)=1/(2j). | Rationalizing gives −j/2, so the imaginary part is −0.5.','The reciprocal of j is −j, not +j.',subject='mathematics',topic='complex-analysis',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('mixed-10','mathematics','linear-algebra','Mixed Revision · Broad ECE Checkpoint','007');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A 3×4 real matrix has rank 2. Find the dimension of its right null space.',2,'Four columns; rank 2','Find nullity of A acting on R⁴.','Rank–nullity theorem',r'\dim\ker A=n-\operatorname{rank}A',r'The input space has dimension equal to the number of columns, 4. | Rank accounts for 2 independent image directions. | Nullity is 4−2=2.','Right-null-space dimension uses columns; the left null space uses rows.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For causal x(t)=e⁻²ᵗu(t), find its unilateral Laplace transform value X(1).',0.3333333333333333,'Exponential rate 2; real transform argument s=1','Evaluate the Laplace integral at the specified s.','Exponential Laplace transform',r'X(s)=\int_0^\infty e^{-(s+2)t}dt',r'At s=1 the integrand becomes e⁻³ᵗ. | The integral over the positive half-line is 1/3. | Thus X(1)=1/3≈0.3333333333.','The transform’s rate combines s and the signal decay coefficient.',subject='networks',topic='laplace-transform')
b.n(r'A machine has 32-bit byte-addressed instructions and 4-byte instruction length. Starting at byte address 100, find the next sequential instruction address.',104,'Byte addressing; current address 100; instruction occupies 4 bytes','Advance by the instruction length.','Program-counter address units',r'PC_{next}=PC+L_{bytes}',r'Byte addressing increments by bytes rather than words. | The instruction consumes addresses 100 through 103. | The next instruction starts at 104.','Bit width of a register does not make instruction addresses count in bits.',subject='digital',topic='computer-organization',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An ideal long-channel MOSFET saturation law is ID=βVov²/2. With fixed β, doubling Vov changes ID by what factor?',4,'Square-law saturation; β unchanged; both biases stay in saturation','Find the current scaling ratio.','Square-law scaling',r'I_{D,2}/I_{D,1}=(V_{ov,2}/V_{ov,1})^2',r'The proportionality coefficient cancels in the ratio. | The overdrive ratio is 2. | Its square is 4, so drain current quadruples under the stated model.','This ideal scaling excludes velocity saturation and output-region changes.',subject='devices',topic='mosfet')
b.m(r'In a stable negative-feedback amplifier with large loop gain, its closed-loop gain is primarily set by:','C',['The open-loop gain alone','The sign of the supply voltage','The feedback factor','The number of transistors alone'],'Stable loop; |Aβ|≫1; negative feedback','Identify the dominant gain-setting network.','High-loop-gain approximation',r'A_f=\frac{A}{1+A\beta}\simeq1/\beta',r'When loop gain is much larger than unity, the 1 term is small relative to Aβ. | The open-loop A approximately cancels. | Closed-loop gain is then chiefly determined by the feedback factor β, option C.','The approximation assumes adequate loop gain and stable linear operation.',subject='analog',topic='feedback')
b.n(r'A causal transfer function H(s)=(s+2)/[(s+1)(s+3)] has a zero at which real value of s?',-2,'Factored numerator and denominator','Find the finite transmission zero.','Transfer-function zeros',r'H(s)=0\iff\text{numerator}=0\quad\text{when denominator}\ne0',r'The numerator vanishes at s=−2. | Neither denominator factor is zero there. | Therefore the finite zero is −2, distinct from poles −1 and −3.','Do not read a denominator root as a transmission zero.',subject='control',topic='transfer-functions')
b.n(r'A 4-PSK transmitter carries uncoded binary data at 12 kbit/s. Find its symbol rate in ksymbol/s.',6,'Four phase symbols; two bits per symbol; no coding overhead','Convert bit rate to symbol rate.','Modulation order and clock',r'R_s=R_b/\log_2M',r'Four symbols represent log₂4=2 bits each. | Divide 12 kbit/s by 2 bits/symbol. | Symbol rate is 6 ksymbol/s.','Higher modulation order changes bits/symbol without by itself specifying BER.',subject='communications',topic='digital-communication',unit='ksymbol/s')
b.n(r'A uniform dielectric has εr=9 and μr=1. What is its refractive index relative to free space under the lossless nondispersive approximation?',3,'Relative constitutive constants 9 and 1','Find c/vp.','Refractive index',r'n=\sqrt{\epsilon_r\mu_r}',r'The phase-speed ratio c/vp is √(εrμr). | Substitute 9×1. | The refractive index is √9=3.','This elementary relation assumes the stated medium model, not an arbitrary dispersive material.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='wave-propagation')
b.s(r'Which statements about causal discrete-time LTI systems with rational H(z) and no pole-zero cancellations are true?',['A','C'],['All poles strictly inside the unit circle imply BIBO stability','A pole at z=1 is strictly BIBO stable','The causal ROC lies outside the outermost pole','All numerator zeros must lie inside the unit circle for BIBO stability'],'Rational causal transfer; standard absolute-summability criterion','Select valid causal ROC and stability conditions.','Discrete-time pole stability',r'|p_i|<1\quad\forall i',r'A causal ROC extends outward from the largest pole magnitude. | It contains the unit circle exactly when every pole is strictly inside that circle. | A boundary pole is not strictly stable; numerator zeros do not impose the claimed BIBO condition. Select A and C.','Minimum-phase requirements on zeros are stronger and different from BIBO stability.',subject='networks',topic='discrete-time-transforms',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For a differentiable real function on an open interval, which statements are valid?',['A','D'],['An interior local extremum has derivative zero','A zero derivative at one point guarantees a local maximum','Every stationary point is a local minimum','A stationary point can be neither a maximum nor a minimum'],'Differentiable function; interior points only','Select necessary and non-sufficient extremum facts.','Stationary-point logic',r'\text{interior extremum}\Rightarrow f\prime(c)=0',r'Fermat’s theorem gives derivative zero at a differentiable interior extremum. | The converse fails: x³ has derivative zero at 0 but is increasing through that point. | Thus stationary points can be inflections rather than extrema; select A and D.','Necessary conditions for an extremum are not sufficient conditions.',subject='mathematics',topic='calculus')
b.n(r'Five independent identical components each work with probability 0.9. A series system works only if all five work. Find its reliability.',0.59049,'Independent components; series success condition; p=0.9','Find joint success probability.','Series reliability',r'R=\prod_{i=1}^5p_i',r'Every component must work simultaneously. | Independence allows multiplication of their five success probabilities. | R=(0.9)⁵=0.59049.','A series system requires intersection, not at least one working component.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude')
b.n(r'For a stable unity-feedback system with G(s)=K/[s(s+2)], find K when its velocity error constant is Kv=5.',10,'Type-1 open loop; Kv=5; stable positive-gain closed loop assumed','Find gain from lim sG(s).','Velocity error constant design',r'K_v=\lim_{s\to0}sG(s)=K/2',r'Cancel the explicit integrator factor before taking the limit. | Kv=K/2=5 gives K=10. | The closed-loop denominator s²+2s+10 has negative-real-part poles, consistent with the stated stability.','The extra pole at −2 contributes its finite DC factor to the velocity constant.',subject='control',topic='time-response',difficulty='hard')
save_batch(banks,'batch-007')
